{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LhPgsDxXSXuibVa22Pt163","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# researchPrime — Best approach to archive symposium.guetta.com\n2\t\n3\t**Mode:** engine-capable (Node v26.1.0, all trio modules imported — import spike `go:true`).\n4\t**Stakes vector:** impact=moderate · reversibility=HIGH (re-runnable downloads) · blast-radius=LOW (authenticated GETs to a site the user legitimately accesses; local writes only) → **governor tier = LOW**. Per the stakes governor, a LOW tier intentionally fires zero debate/Synthesizer/Judge sub-agents; the dominant uncertainties here are **empirical** (resolved at first authenticated contact), not adjudicable by debate.\n5\t**ISOLATION:** approximated (single-context).\n6\t**Honesty stamp:** schema conforms; a full multi-round adversarial swarm did NOT run — deliberately down-scaled by the LOW stakes tier. Findings below are OBSERVED (live curl probes) except where marked.\n7\t\n8\t## What the target actually is (OBSERVED via curl)\n9\t- `https://symposium.guetta.com/` → **307 redirect to `/auth/login`**; backend is **Flask on Railway behind Cloudflare**.\n10\t- Auth = a **signed `session` cookie** (`itsdangerous` format `payload.ts.hmac`; flags `httponly; secure; samesite=lax; Max-Age=86400`). All authenticated access hinges on this one cookie.\n11\t- Pages observed (login, 404) are **server-rendered HTML**, not an empty JS shell — a good sign that a plain HTTP mirror can discover content URLs. Static assets served under `/static/...`.\n12\t- **Cloudflare did NOT bot-challenge plain curl** (clean 307/404/login responses, no `cf-chl`/JS challenge). → an HTTP-level mirror is viable, not blocked.\n13\t- Host tooling: `curl.exe` ✓, **Python 3.13 + `requests` + `pypdf` + `playwright`** ✓. No `yt-dlp`/`wget`/`ffmpeg`.\n14\t- Direct cookie extraction from Chrome's store is **blocked by the permission classifier** (credential-store access) → auth handoff must be **user-mediated**.\n15\t\n16\t## Expected structure (from Schedule v6.pdf — extracted)\n17\tOne-day symposium \"The MBA Transformed? AI and Beyond\", Columbia Business School, June 1 2026. ~14 timed sessions, most with 2–4 named speakers + two \"Mini-presentations\" blocks. The live site (`daniel.guetta.com/symposium`) mirrors this outline — used as the completeness checklist for the archive.\n18\t\n19\t## Recommended approach — two-track, start cheap\n20\t**Track 1 (preferred): authenticated HTTP mirror.** User supplies the live `session` cookie → Python `requests` session crawls `/auth`-gated pages, parses every link to `.mp4/.pdf/.pptx/.key` + `/static` assets, downloads with resume, and reconstructs the schedule into a folder tree. Cheapest; already de-risked (curl gets clean responses, pages are server-rendered, no CF challenge).\n21\t**Track 2 (auto-fallback): Playwright drive of a user-logged-in browser.** I launch Chromium, user logs in once, the script renders each page, captures network (incl. any video manifests), and downloads. Robust to: JS/SPA-rendered content, HLS-segmented or signed/expiring video URLs, and mid-run cookie expiry.\n22\t\n23\tDecision rule: try Track 1; if an authenticated page returns a JS shell, or videos are HLS/`blob:`/signed, switch that portion to Track 2.\n24\t\n25\t## Foresight receipt (dropped/reordered branches + counterfactual cost)\n26\t- **Dropped: decrypt Chrome's cookie DB myself.** Counterfactual cost ≈ 0 — it's blocked by policy AND fragile under Chrome App-Bound Encryption; user-paste/Playwright dominate it.\n27\t- **Reordered: Playwright-first → Track 2 (fallback).** Counterfactual cost of leading with it = a `playwright install` browser download + heavier setup for content that curl probes suggest is plain authenticated files. Cheaper to try the HTTP mirror first.\n28\t- **Dropped: `yt-dlp` for videos.** Not installed; only needed if videos turn out to be HLS — defer install to that branch rather than pay it upfront.\n29\t\n30\t## Residual risks (flagged, resolved at execution)\n31\t1. Authenticated front-end could still be client-rendered (login page wasn't) → Track 2.\n32\t2. Videos could be HLS/signed/expiring → detect on first video; Track 2 + maybe `yt-dlp`/`ffmpeg`.\n33\t3. `session` Max-Age 86400 but post-login lifetime unknown → download videos first, or use Track 2 for re-auth.\n34\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30ae663e-069a-4e09-b719-86f9e60a79e8","uuid":"9e50114a-1842-4c94-8c27-0c71eda4cfc4","timestamp":"2026-06-23T18:23:09.685Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\MBA Teaching AI\\.research-symposium\\RESEARCH_REPORT.md","content":"# researchPrime — Best approach to archive symposium.guetta.com\n\n**Mode:** engine-capable (Node v26.1.0, all trio modules imported — import spike `go:true`).\n**Stakes vector:** impact=moderate · reversibility=HIGH (re-runnable downloads) · blast-radius=LOW (authenticated GETs to a site the user legitimately accesses; local writes only) → **governor tier = LOW**. Per the stakes governor, a LOW tier intentionally fires zero debate/Synthesizer/Judge sub-agents; the dominant uncertainties here are **empirical** (resolved at first authenticated contact), not adjudicable by debate.\n**ISOLATION:** approximated (single-context).\n**Honesty stamp:** schema conforms; a full multi-round adversarial swarm did NOT run — deliberately down-scaled by the LOW stakes tier. Findings below are OBSERVED (live curl probes) except where marked.\n\n## What the target actually is (OBSERVED via curl)\n- `https://symposium.guetta.com/` → **307 redirect to `/auth/login`**; backend is **Flask on Railway behind Cloudflare**.\n- Auth = a **signed `session` cookie** (`itsdangerous` format `payload.ts.hmac`; flags `httponly; secure; samesite=lax; Max-Age=86400`). All authenticated access hinges on this one cookie.\n- Pages observed (login, 404) are **server-rendered HTML**, not an empty JS shell — a good sign that a plain HTTP mirror can discover content URLs. Static assets served under `/static/...`.\n- **Cloudflare did NOT bot-challenge plain curl** (clean 307/404/login responses, no `cf-chl`/JS challenge). → an HTTP-level mirror is viable, not blocked.\n- Host tooling: `curl.exe` ✓, **Python 3.13 + `requests` + `pypdf` + `playwright`** ✓. No `yt-dlp`/`wget`/`ffmpeg`.\n- Direct cookie extraction from Chrome's store is **blocked by the permission classifier** (credential-store access) → auth handoff must be **user-mediated**.\n\n## Expected structure (from Schedule v6.pdf — extracted)\nOne-day symposium \"The MBA Transformed? AI and Beyond\", Columbia Business School, June 1 2026. ~14 timed sessions, most with 2–4 named speakers + two \"Mini-presentations\" blocks. The live site (`daniel.guetta.com/symposium`) mirrors this outline — used as the completeness checklist for the archive.\n\n## Recommended approach — two-track, start cheap\n**Track 1 (preferred): authenticated HTTP mirror.** User supplies the live `session` cookie → Python `requests` session crawls `/auth`-gated pages, parses every link to `.mp4/.pdf/.pptx/.key` + `/static` assets, downloads with resume, and reconstructs the schedule into a folder tree. Cheapest; already de-risked (curl gets clean responses, pages are server-rendered, no CF challenge).\n**Track 2 (auto-fallback): Playwright drive of a user-logged-in browser.** I launch Chromium, user logs in once, the script renders each page, captures network (incl. any video manifests), and downloads. Robust to: JS/SPA-rendered content, HLS-segmented or signed/expiring video URLs, and mid-run cookie expiry.\n\nDecision rule: try Track 1; if an authenticated page returns a JS shell, or videos are HLS/`blob:`/signed, switch that portion to Track 2.\n\n## Foresight receipt (dropped/reordered branches + counterfactual cost)\n- **Dropped: decrypt Chrome's cookie DB myself.** Counterfactual cost ≈ 0 — it's blocked by policy AND fragile under Chrome App-Bound Encryption; user-paste/Playwright dominate it.\n- **Reordered: Playwright-first → Track 2 (fallback).** Counterfactual cost of leading with it = a `playwright install` browser download + heavier setup for content that curl probes suggest is plain authenticated files. Cheaper to try the HTTP mirror first.\n- **Dropped: `yt-dlp` for videos.** Not installed; only needed if videos turn out to be HLS — defer install to that branch rather than pay it upfront.\n\n## Residual risks (flagged, resolved at execution)\n1. Authenticated front-end could still be client-rendered (login page wasn't) → Track 2.\n2. Videos could be HLS/signed/expiring → detect on first video; Track 2 + maybe `yt-dlp`/`ffmpeg`.\n3. `session` Max-Age 86400 but post-login lifetime unknown → download videos first, or use Track 2 for re-auth.\n","numLines":34,"startLine":1,"totalLines":34}}}
