ve to the trust it advertises. Two directly-observed, reader-irreconcilable numeric contradictions and several out-of-chain claims live in the Dean-facing brief — the one document where a catchable error would most damage the 'verified' credibility the whole effort trades on. Fixable with a reconcile-or-disclose pass and a scoped provenance note before circulation.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F1\",\n      \"kind\": \"diagnose\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cross-document numeric inconsistency between two shipped deliverables. CAiSEY: full summary says '4,100+ students across 40+ courses and ~23–25 institutions'; Executive Summary says '~3,000 students across 10+ schools.' Ipeirotis oral exams: full summary says '$3 per 30-minute exam (~11¢/student-minute)'; Executive Summary says '36 students, ~25 min each, ~$15 of compute total' — $3×36≈$108 cannot equal $15, and 30 min ≠ 25 min. Both numbers are read directly from the PDFs (OBSERVED). A reader cannot reconcile them from the documents, and the divergence sits in the Dean-facing brief, maximizing reputational cost. Scoped as unreconcilable-by-the-reader rather than proven factual error, because the figures could in principle be the same metric at different dates.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Reconcile every quantitative claim in the exec brief against the grounded full summary; where a number cannot be reconciled, cite both with timepoints/source or drop the false precision, before the brief circulates.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F2\",\n      \"kind\": \"diagnose\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Scope-of-trust mismatch. The prominent verification stamp ('verified transcripts ... ≈92–98% agreement ... proper nouns reconciled against slides') certifies transcription fidelity only. It does not certify (a) the factual truth of speaker claims, nor (b) claims added in the synthesis layer