{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":336948,"duration_api_ms":337703,"ttft_ms":12976,"ttft_stream_ms":3020,"time_to_request_ms":81,"num_turns":18,"result":"I have enough grounded material. I read the two principal synthesis deliverables (the 8-page transcript-grounded summary and the Dean-facing Executive Summary), the research/scrape pipeline (`.research-symposium/`, with its own `RESEARCH_REPORT.md` documenting an authenticated-HTTP-mirror methodology), and the project's Anchor metadata. Two cross-document defects are directly observable, the coverage-honesty of the full summary is a genuine strength, and the `.research-symposium/` working tree (untracked per `git status`, not ignored) contains a `session` cookie / `sess.json` / auth logs.\n\nEmitting the RAW draft per the runtime contract (no self-assigned tiers/stamps — the host's seam pass applies them):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": \"gandalf.raw-draft.v1\",\n  \"cross_model\": false,\n  \"degraded\": true,\n  \"degraded_legs\": [\n    {\n      \"leg\": \"situate\",\n      \"why\": \"researchPrime was NOT commissioned in this single-context Tier-1 run. The one situate elevation below is a candidate cross-field frame offered as needs_verification, not a CORROBORATED/OBSERVED fact. Any best-in-class framing is UNVERIFIED.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"leg\": \"substrate\",\n      \"why\": \"Single-family host (cross_model:false). Refuter/judge are a conservative lower bound; GROUNDED is unreachable here — PROMISING is the ceiling.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reasoning\": \"TRIAGE. The artifact is MIXED: (a) a data-collection pipeline under .research-symposium/ that authenticates to symposium.guetta.com, mirrors slides/videos, transcribes with Whisper large-v3, cross-checks against Vimeo auto-captions, and (b) a set of synthesized deliverables — an 8-page session-by-session 'Symposium Summary', a 1-page Dean-facing 'Executive Summary', a supplemental report, a Columbia teaching brief, and a standalone Ipeirotis package. The load-bearing claim of the whole effort is a TRUST claim, stamped explicitly: 'drawn from verified transcripts ... ≈92–98% agreement ... proper nouns reconciled against slides.' Stakes = medium: an internal influence artifact that carries the author's credibility and recommends real resource allocation (a summer faculty study group, ordered course list). Depth = FULL.\\n\\nDEEP STRUCTURE. This is a chain-of-custody / provenance artifact: recording -> transcript -> full summary -> exec brief. Its value rests on fidelity across that chain. The type-appropriate lenses are therefore provenance integrity, internal consistency, coverage-honesty, and inferential restraint.\\n\\nDIAGNOSIS. The full 8-page summary is, on its own, SOUND and high quality: it is faithful to program order, synthesizes five non-trivial cross-cutting threads, and is unusually honest about coverage gaps (Kyle Jensen 'slides only — not recorded'; Brad Sturt and Song Ma 'no recording or slides posted'). A naive verdict would stop at 'this is sound.' The VERDICT-INVERTING reading: the trust stamp certifies transcription FIDELITY (Whisper vs Vimeo agreement on what was said), not the factual TRUTH of speakers' claims, and — critically — it does not reach the synthesis/exec layer that actually drives decisions. Three checkable defects sit in exactly that ungoverned layer. (1) The Dean-facing Executive Summary contradicts the grounded full summary on quantitative facts: CAiSEY is '~3,000 students across 10+ schools' (exec) vs '4,100+ students across 40+ courses and ~23–25 institutions' (full); Ipeirotis is '36 students, ~25 min each, ~$15 of compute total' (exec) vs '$3 per 30-minute exam' (full) — $3 × 36 ≈ $108, irreconcilable with $15. A reader cannot reconcile these from the documents. (2) The exec brief asserts specifics about talks the full summary flags as having NO posted recording/slides (Song Ma's 'Agent CXOs', Brad Sturt's 'AgentOps course with an AI-judged pitch competition') and adds an external citation (Martin 'featured in The Wall Street Journal,' Nov. 2025) — claims outside the verified-transcript chain, presented with the same authority. (3) Speaker assertions (10,000× startup-cost drop, an 'Anthropic study' showing a 17% mastery drop, R² 0.10->0.50) are relayed as established fact; acceptable in an archive, riskier once the exec brief leans on them to justify recommendations.\\n\\nDISCONFIRMATION (named): the divergent numbers MIGHT be the same metric captured at different dates (the talk vs a later write-up), i.e. an update rather than a contradiction. I cannot confirm reconciliation from the artifact, so the finding is scoped as 'unreconcilable-by-the-reader,' not 'factual error.'\\n\\nOPERATOR-WRONG pass. The operator is evidently proud of, and has invested heavily in, the verification rigor (a whole transcription/cross-check pipeline, a prominent 'verified transcripts' stamp). The uncomfortable finding against that preference: the verification effort is concentrated where it was EASIEST and least likely to be wrong (what words were spoken, name spelling) and is ABSENT where it is hardest and most consequential (the Dean-facing numbers, external citations, and un-posted-talk specifics). The elaborate transcription pipeline risks functioning as rigor-theater — a high-effort guarantee on a low-risk stage — while the layer that moves a Dean ships unreconciled figures next to the word 'verified.' A separate, smaller operator-blind spot: the .research-symposium/ working tree is untracked-but-NOT-ignored (per git status it appears as '??', so a single 'git add -A' would stage it) and contains an authenticated session cookie (cookies.json, sess.json) and auth-capture logs.\\n\\nVerdict is stated after this reasoning.\",\n  \"verdict\": \"The full Symposium Summary is sound, faithful, and admirably honest about its coverage gaps — return that plainly. But the effort is NOT uniformly sound: t