ims, nor (b) claims added in the synthesis layer. The Executive Summary asserts specifics about talks the full summary explicitly flags as having NO posted recording or slides (Song Ma's 'Agent CXOs'; Brad Sturt's 'AgentOps course with an AI-judged pitch competition') and introduces an external citation (Martin in The Wall Street Journal, Nov. 2025) — all carried with the same authority as the verified material. The trust guarantee thus stops two transformations short of the artifact the decision-maker actually consumes.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"Attach a scope note to the 'verified' stamp (what it does and does NOT certify) and mark every exec-brief claim that is not transcript-grounded as separately sourced; verify the WSJ citation and the un-posted-talk specifics or downgrade them to 'per program abstract / unconfirmed.'\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F3\",\n      \"kind\": \"diagnose\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speaker assertions are relayed as established fact without epistemic distancing: the ~10,000× drop in startup-launch cost, 'an Anthropic study finding AI assistance cut quiz mastery 17%,' CAiSEY's 4,100+/40+/23–25 figures, R² rising 0.10->0.50. This is appropriate and expected for a faithful archive (the summary's job is to report what was said). It becomes a risk only where the exec brief reuses these figures to justify a recommendation, because the reader inherits the speaker's optimism as if it were the author's verified finding.\",\n      \"verdict\": \"In the archive, leave as-is (correct for the genre). In the exec brief, attribute load-bearing statistics to the speaker ('Griffel cited ~10,000×') rather than stating them in the document's own voice.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"F4\",\n      \"kind\": \"diagnose\",\n      \"severity\": \"info\",\n      \"rung\": \"OBSERVED\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Named strength (Channel-2 'this is sound' on the core artifact). The full summary ha